\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The two internal columns modulo three}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For #{p=3}, the choice #{a=b=1} in
\ref{fdip-00VU} gives exactly the matrices
#{Q_{\qi},Q_{\qj}} of \ref{fdip-00V7}. The image of #{h}
is the displayed #{Q_h} there. A coefficient matrix
#{Z=(z_1,z_2)} therefore identifies the
#{4}-dimensional Hurwitz fiber with two copies
of #{S=\mathbb F_3^2}. Left multiplication acts
on both columns by the same matrix. The
reduced alternating form in \ref{fdip-00V8} pairs these
columns through its explicit invertible
multiplicity matrix. The coefficient module and
the form on its columns remain different data.}
